<p>In the latest turn in the U.S. war with Iran, the Pentagon has prepared a request for more than $200 billion in additional funding, though as of March 19 the White House had not formally sent it to Congress. The proposal is already running into resistance on Capitol Hill, where Democrats and some Republicans are asking why such a large sum is needed after sizable defense appropriations last year. Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth said U.S. objectives in the war have not changed since strikes began on Feb. 28, even as the conflict’s effects spread well beyond Washington’s budget fight. PBS reported that Iranian strikes on oil and gas facilities across the Persian Gulf came a day after Israel struck Iran’s main natural gas complex, jolting energy markets and pushing up gasoline prices in the United States as President Trump tried to reassure Americans.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/XGKIVe0dWV5Z27hNQZpskXWsmEJ5dyT4PMspPyW_URKc0E_nhfWv3SDxecIYhrr2lcYUAGHvMHVFNVhnbQyCnj2HNvGriGOlH76zACUkuyNLW892CeL8JozcU8farid2HoDtGr-zXGy5IviJ8Kp5abFet3LIQkOn1jMsGrZu0vyZClqx6a-Qba_lJULKVBqbW0Aisi0paXTnqTpqGAmm2SxeuxuOBYIIyL7P2RCkMjEqpKvuHRsDvp-4F3HO3UPg4brRDrSE1tdQp8w5V_jYN_QZO64doxyzuiJciJ5_PSb8edqG960mkIztpA3Ycm_066PdLzXxWIfPeQTwOklHZxYnVmKDPo6S1pMTW07-qQQpWW-iev_9azee7igDpREnofTHQLQN2zFBy8QiYcRjbnEdttHYSyH0MhySjGC9wbeN88mu-N1mmRHUFKj1qYxjtqtHU0zCbI_AxZ3sdvj8w84468iD7iUHshqzJAuSAUdNZ021wyc' alt='Image accompanying coverage of the Pentagon&#x27;s request for additional Iran war funding.' /><br /><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pentagon: The administration’s funding push remains in an internal stage, with the Defense Department seeking White House approval and formal transmittal to Congress before lawmakers can act. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/world/middleeast/pentagon-200-billion-iran-war-funding-hegseth.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Congressional critics: Skeptical lawmakers are framing the fight as a matter of fiscal oversight, arguing that last year’s 